#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gh-post — rate-limit-proof GitHub posting for fleet writes that matter.

Drew's rule (2026-11-05, AGENTS.md): WHEN GITHUB RATE-LIMITS A POST, USE THE
OUTBOX / THE GITHUB APP. Origin failure: the code#1258 Q3/Q6 ruling comment
failed to post twice on the gh secondary rate limit (2026-11-03) and sat in a
prepared file for two days. Design + proofs: developing-today/code#6129.

The ladder (first success wins):
  1. USER  — `gh` as dezren39, verified by readback of the posted object.
  2. APP   — the developing.today GitHub App (App ID 5454422) via
             infra/fleet-paperwork/fleet_paperwork.py on the laptop. An
             installation token is a different actor with its own rate-limit
             bucket. fleet-paperwork verifies by readback itself. Only
             attempted when leg 1 failed with a rate-limit-class error.
  3. OUTBOX — a fully prepared entry in the fleet action outbox
             (~/workspace/goals/keep-the-four-repos-healthy/hidden_files/
             action-outbox.md, protocol of 2026-10-28). The
             action-outbox-processor cron (30m) is the pusher: it executes
             the entry exactly as prepared, verifies, and records the URL.
             gh-post prints "QUEUED OB-<date>-<nnn>" and exits 3 — queued is
             a result, never a silent file.

Non-rate-limit failures (bad target, missing file, auth) fail loudly at the
leg where they occur; they never fall through to the App or the outbox.

Usage:
  gh-post comment --repo owner/name (--issue N | --pr N) --body-file F
  gh-post create  --repo owner/name --title T --body-file F [--label L ...]
  gh-post note    --repo owner/name (--issue N | --pr N) --body-file F
                  [--note-key K]
  gh-post check   --body-file F        (pre-post secret scan only)

Live notes (dezren39/hatch#13, spec item 5 — structural reduction):
`gh-post note` maintains ONE live note per (target, note key): the
first call creates the comment, later calls EDIT it in place via the
API, so a task's checkpoint trail is a single comment instead of one
comment per checkpoint. Target: <= 2 comments per task (the claim
comment + the live note). The note key defaults to $FLEET_JOB (or
"gh-post"); the note carries a <!-- fleet-live-note:<key> --> marker.
Note edits are ledgered as creations (the design's open question 2:
assume PATCH counts toward the budget until proven otherwise).

Pre-post secret scan (code#7072, 2026-11-09): every comment/create body is
scanned BEFORE any posting leg. Credential-shaped text — env-dump shapes
(many KEY=value lines), known token prefixes, credential KEY=value lines,
private-key blocks — blocks the post with exit 4 and a finding list that
names the pattern and line, never the value. Origin: 2026-11-07 lane
code-e posted an env dump as a comment via an unquoted heredoc (shell
expanded $VARS from a body file built with <<EOF). Post bodies must be
built with quoted heredocs (<<'EOF') or body files only; gh-post is the
sanctioned posting path and this scan is its structural backstop.

Test hooks (env): GH_POST_SKIP_USER=1 skips leg 1 as if rate-limited;
GH_POST_SKIP_APP=1 skips leg 2 as if it failed. Both together drive the
outbox leg end-to-end.

Content ledger (dezren39/hatch#3, phase 1 of the content-creation
throttle; design code#2013 / PR code#2226, tracker code#2227): every
post reserves a row in the shared creation ledger
(~/.local/state/fleet/content-ledger.sqlite) BEFORE the first leg runs
and finalises it once the outcome is known — created (a leg posted and
a read confirmed it), or failed with the reason (including "queued to
the outbox", which is not a creation). The posted body carries a
<!-- fleet-nonce:… --> marker so a create whose output is lost can
still be verified by search. If the ledger cannot be written, gh-post
refuses to post at all (exit 5): unledgered filing is exactly the
silent-success failure mode the ledger exists to kill.

Content throttle (dezren39/hatch#13): every post also passes the
shared throttle gate (tools/content-throttle) BEFORE the ledger
reservation. The throttle ships warn-only — it logs what it would have
delayed and delays nothing — until the mode is flipped after the
one-week review (see tools/content-throttle/README.md). The circuit
breaker is live in both modes: if the circuit is open, the user leg is
treated as rate-limited WITHOUT being attempted (no spinning against
a refusing API), and the ladder continues with the App leg / outbox.
User-leg results feed the breaker: a post clears the failure count, a
rate-limit counts toward the five that open the circuit. If the
throttle itself is unavailable, gh-post refuses to post (exit 5) —
never a silent fall-back to unthrottled filing.
"""

